On Tue, Jun 10, 2014 at 3:05 AM, Dieter Menne
<menne...@googlemail.com> wrote:
> Again, the requirement to install Haskell/Pandoc from source was a wrong
> reading of the message that version 1.12 of Pandoc was required. It turned
> out that Pandoc was not found at all, but:
>
> -- Under Windows/runApp everything ok
> -- Under Ubuntu/runApp everything ok (!sic, I had not tested that before).
> So it means that Pandoc was found, and RStdudi also reports the correct
> RSTUDIO_PANDOC
> -- Under Ubuntu-shinyServer, the 1.12 is required message comes up.
>
> So again, it is the same story as with non-Ascii characters in the seperate
> thread you replied to: shiny-server does not pick up the environment.
>
> The following helps, but it could lead to problems.
>
> # global.R
> if (!nzchar(Sys.getenv("RSTUDIO_PANDOC")))
>   Sys.setenv(RSTUDIO_PANDOC = "/usr/lib/rstudio-server/bin/pandoc")
>
> Using a symlink works too, but I do not recommend this method. Your Pandoc
> is not standard and seems to work only with render/rmarkdown. Simply try -v
> or --version. So a global symlink could lead to problems in other parts of
> the system.
>
> Dieter

